{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-214247",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${2}^{2}+{2}^{2}≰4.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
